WF-41 — Admin → User Relay

## Summary

- Inputs: WF-10 Relay Envelope (design.md §2.2): { phoneNumber, messageText, user:{id,name,phone_number,status,slack_channel_id,current_consultation_id}, adminUserId, channelId, channelName } — carried verbatim from WF-10's `Build WF-10 Relay Envelope` Code node (Step 23 in WF-10.pseudo). No re-derivation needed.
- Outputs: WhatsApp text (in-window) OR utility-template (out-window) to user via WF-50
- State Transitions: none
- Calls Sub-Workflows: WF-50 (Send WhatsApp)
- **Notes:**
  - WF-41 is intentionally single-direction (admin → user). The reverse direction (user's WhatsApp message → admin's Slack channel) is handled by **WF-40 User → Admin Relay** (called from WF-02 via the `RELAY` route). Do not re-introduce WA→Slack handling here.
  - Caller contract: WF-10 has already (a) looked up the user by `slack_channel_id`, (b) verified `status == 'consultation_active'`, and (c) shaped the canonical Relay Envelope via the `Build WF-10 Relay Envelope` Code node (design.md §2.2). WF-41 does NOT re-query users or re-derive phone — that would be redundant work and a duplicated state-precondition surface.
  - **Field name:** the admin's message body arrives as `messageText` in the Relay Envelope (canonical per §2.2). WF-41 maps it to `messageContent` when calling WF-50 (canonical WF-50 text variant per design.md §2.3).
  - **24h-window deliverability (PDF-15, spec 2026-06-08-24h-window-deliverability-design.md, DD-A/B/C/D):** Meta only permits free-form (non-template) messages inside the customer's open 24h service window (M1); outside it, free-form is silently rejected. WF-41 therefore reads the customer's window state and routes the relay reply accordingly — in-window free-form (unchanged), out-window via the `astrology_service_update_v2` utility template.
  - **Out-window template (PDF-21, 2026-06-09): `astrology_service_update_v2`** (replaced `astrology_service_update`, retired by the user for a Meta-UI bold-rendering bug — the old body's split `*` rendered literal asterisks). v2 structure (lang `en`): Header TEXT fixed *"Follow-up on your consultation"*; Body *"\*Dr. Chinmay has responded to your message:\* {{1}}\n\nThanks, Chinmay Astro"* — **one positional body var `{{1}}` = the DD-C-sanitized reply**; no footer var, no buttons. Send-payload contract is UNCHANGED from the old template (single body text param), so only the template NAME moved in `Prepare WhatsApp Message` (`const TEMPLATE`).
  - **Window source (DD-B):** `chinmay_astro.messages` — `MAX(created_at) WHERE user_id=<envelope user.id> AND direction='inbound' AND metadata->>'transport'='wa'`. The relay path does no `users` write, so `users.last_message_at` is stale during a consultation and is NOT used. The 24h comparison is computed in SQL (timestamptz vs `NOW()`) so it is timezone-correct and does not depend on n8n's runtime TZ.
  - **Transport scope (PDF-20, 2026-06-09):** the window read MUST filter `metadata->>'transport'='wa'`. `messages` logs Dr. Chinmay's own Slack typing as `direction='inbound'` `message_type='slack_text'` (`transport='slack'`); without the transport filter a Slack-inbound row can become the `MAX(inbound)` the gate keys on, so the window reads freshly-open off the astrologer's message while the customer has actually been silent >24h → relay wrongly sends free-form → Meta rejects out-of-window → silent non-delivery (the exact failure PDF-15 was built to prevent). `transport='wa'` is the correct discriminator (NOT a `message_type` allow-list): the 24h window re-opens on ANY customer WhatsApp inbound — text, interactive, OR media — so filtering by transport counts them all, whereas a `message_type IN ('text','interactive')` proxy would under-count a future media inbound. Mirrors WF-75's window read.
  - **Stateless gate (DD-2/DD-3):** each relay message is evaluated fresh against the current window state. A message that was sent as a template (or that later fails at Meta) has NO effect on how the next message is processed — there is no rejection memory, no per-customer "blocked relay" state.
  - **No bounce-back (DD-C):** out-window replies are pre-processed to template-safe form and delivered. There is no "shorten and resend" path back to Dr. Chinmay; any residual Meta send failure (paused template, rate limit) is surfaced separately by PDF-16 (the failure-visibility backstop).
  - **Read-source convention:** after the Postgres window read, `$json` is the query result (last_inbound / in_window), NOT the relay envelope. The payload-build step reads window state from `$json` and reads the envelope fields from the always-run trigger node `$('When Executed by Another Workflow')` — never from the Postgres output.

---

## Algorithm

Step 1: Start — triggered by WF-10 (`Call WF-41 (Admin->User Relay)`) with the canonical Relay Envelope: { phoneNumber, messageText, user, adminUserId, channelId, channelName }.

Step 2: Load customer window state (Postgres node "Load Last Inbound", alwaysOutputData=true):
        SELECT MAX(created_at) AS last_inbound,
               (MAX(created_at) > NOW() - INTERVAL '24 hours') AS in_window
          FROM chinmay_astro.messages
         WHERE user_id = <envelope user.id> AND direction = 'inbound'
           AND metadata->>'transport' = 'wa';   -- PDF-20: WhatsApp inbound only; excludes Slack-inbound (astrologer typing) skew
        - in_window = TRUE  → customer's 24h service window is open.
        - in_window = FALSE → window closed.
        - 0 inbound rows → last_inbound = NULL, in_window = NULL → treated as window-CLOSED in Step 3
          (a customer with no logged inbound never opened a window; the template path always delivers).

Step 3: Build WhatsApp payload(s) (Code node "Prepare WhatsApp Message").
        Reads window state from $json (Step 2 result) and the envelope from $('When Executed by Another Workflow').
        → IF in_window === true:  (window OPEN — unchanged free-form behaviour, free per M1/M2)
            emit ONE item:
              { phoneNumber, messageType: "text", messageContent: <envelope messageText> }
        → ELSE: (window CLOSED or unknown — out-window template path, DD-C)
            sanitize = template-safe transform of <envelope messageText> per M4:
              replace newlines/tabs with a single space; collapse runs of 2+ spaces to one; trim.
            IF sanitize.length <= 900:
              emit ONE template item (below) with templateParams = [sanitize].
            ELSE:
              split `sanitize` into N parts of <= ~880 chars on word boundaries; prefix each part with
              "(i/N) "; emit N template items, one per part, templateParams = [<labelled part>].
            each template item:
              { phoneNumber,
                messageType:  "template",
                templateName: "astrology_service_update_v2",
                templateParams: [ <sanitized reply, or labelled part> ],
                userId:         <envelope user.id>,            // logging context (WF-60)
                consultationId: <envelope user.current_consultation_id> }

Step 4: Call WF-50 (Send WhatsApp) with the payload item(s), executeWorkflow mode='each' (run WF-50
        once per input item). WF-50's entry guard validates the discriminated union (design.md §2.3),
        routes on messageType (text → send free-form; template → POST a `astrology_service_update_v2`
        template with components built from templateParams, language en), and logs each send via WF-60
        (direction='outbound'). mode='each' is REQUIRED for the out-window multi-part case: WF-50's
        internal nodes all read `$input.first()` / `return [{json}]`, so a single mode='once' call passing
        N items would collapse to the FIRST item only and silently drop parts 2..N. mode='each' runs WF-50
        once per part → N template sends, N log rows, parts delivered in (1/N) order. In-window emits a
        single item → one run, identical to prior behaviour. WF-41 does not consume the return.

Step 5: End.
